<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Riga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Riga (1921), Treaty of Riga · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-soviet-war">Polish–Soviet War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of Riga was signed on 18 March 1921 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on one side and Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> on the other, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-soviet-war">Polish–Soviet War</a>. It fixed a border that left western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and western Ukraine to Poland, and it remained in force until the Soviet invasion of Poland in 1939.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga.jpg" alt="Peace of Riga" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: official document of Poland · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peace_treaty_of_Riga_1921.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
